# Thinkers maps — introduce the argument

Who is on the page is an argument judgment, not a demographic slot. The Western male canon is not the unmarked default. Gender, race, and nation are not why someone is here, and not how they are introduced — unless that identity **is** the thesis.

## Copy

Lead with the work and the quarrel. Inclusion is natural, never announced.

Keep identity language only when the claim is about sex, reproduction, race, coloniality, nation, or land (Beauvoir, Irigaray, Federici, Firestone, Spillers, Mills, Fanon, Davis, Wynter; Moreton-Robinson as Goenpul of the Quandamooka nation because the land is the claim).

## Do not

- Census named women or write ratio notes in frame notes / timelines
- Add a name to balance a headcount (“matching woman”, “woman hinge”)
- Introduce someone as the missing woman, the other woman, a diversity annex/sticker, or a non-European entry point
- Park figures on a segregated “diversity” / “decolonial” annex instead of the map that asks the question

## Who belongs

Add someone when they change the quarrel of that current. Wire them in prose, edges, timeline, and currents tags. Do not erase the European archive to make a ratio look even. Same rule for writer picks on `fiction-of-the-maps.html`.

## Good vs bad

```text
❌ BAD — “Copjec is the other woman this leap still needs on the page.”
✅ GOOD — Copjec’s orthopsychic subject / Read My Desire is the 1989 half of the Lacan leap.

❌ BAD — “Luxemburg is the woman missing from the founding pair.”
✅ GOOD — Luxemburg’s accumulation thesis sits beside, not inside, the later Frankfurt archive.

❌ BAD — add three Warwick men, then a woman so the census moves.
✅ GOOD — add Chun because Programmed Visions is the software-studies foil on Technics.
```
